    "comments": [
      {
        "id": "comment-1",
        "text": "VECTORS\n\nA 3D vector is three decimals (x, y, z). Make Vector3 builds one from three numbers; the vector\nnodes add, take the dot / cross product and the length (magnitude). Vector2 nodes do the same in 2D.\nA vector is ONE value, so it travels along a single wire.\n  a = (1, 2, 3)   b = (4, 5, 6)   a + b = (5, 7, 9)   a . b = 1*4 + 2*5 + 3*6 = 32\nClass variables hold single values (numbers, text, booleans), so the collection itself lives on the wires between nodes and only the answers are stored in variables.",
        "position": [
          60.0,
          -300.0
        ],
        "size": [
          900.0,
          230.0
        ],
        "color": [
          0.2,
          0.4,
          0.8,
          0.3
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": []
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-2",
        "text": "MAKE\nTwo Make Vector3 nodes build a = (1,2,3) and b = (4,5,6). Each output wire carries a whole vector.",
        "position": [
          60.0,
          460.0
        ],
        "size": [
          640.0,
          90.0
        ],
        "color": [
          0.2,
          0.65,
          0.35,
          0.3
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": [
          "a",
          "b"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-3",
        "text": "DOT, LENGTH, ADD\nDot product 1*4 + 2*5 + 3*6 = 32. Magnitude of a = sqrt(14) ~ 3.742. Vector3 Add gives (5, 7, 9) and Get Vector Z reads its third component: 9.",
        "position": [
          360.0,
          1050.0
        ],
        "size": [
          1260.0,
          100.0
        ],
        "color": [
          0.9,
          0.55,
          0.15,
          0.3
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": [
          "dotn",
          "mag",
          "add",
          "getz"
        ]
      }
    ],
